From ca27e77f64827d916d73816c67a0b66b26033599 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lajosh=C3=A1zi=2C=20L=C3=A1szl=C3=B3=20Gergely?=
 <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 23:23:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] cpuidle: psci: run the init after deferred probing has been
 flushed
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

psci_idle_init() creates a faux device, which is probed synchronously and
has no way to ask for a retry. Its probe attaches every CPU to the "psci"
power domain, so it can only succeed once the CPU PM domain provider in
cpuidle-psci-domain.c has itself probed.

That provider is a platform driver and may be deferred - it is, as soon as
the CPU cluster domains are children of a domain whose driver probes later,
such as an always-on wakeup controller. The deferred probe is retried by
deferred_probe_initcall(), a late_initcall, so a device_initcall here runs
first, fails with -EPROBE_DEFER inside the faux probe, and cpuidle is then
lost for the lifetime of the boot: the CPUs fall back to plain WFI and no
idle state is registered at all.

Move the init to late_initcall_sync so it runs after that flush. On a board
where nothing defers, this only makes the same successful init happen a
little later in boot.

Fixes: af5376a77e87 ("cpuidle: psci: Transition to the faux device interface")
Signed-off-by: Lajosházi, László Gergely <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
---

Tool disclosure, per Documentation/process/generated-content.rst:

Produced with the assistance of Claude (claude-opus-5) driven from Claude Code,
under my direction, while adding a wakeup-interrupt-controller node above the
CPU cluster power domains on msm8953 (sdm632, Fairphone 3) and finding that
cpuidle had disappeared entirely. The assistant read the boot log ordering and
the two drivers, identified the faux-device/deferred-probe race, and drafted
this patch and its message. I reviewed both.

The tool-affected portion is the whole of this patch and all of the commit
message.

Testing: built and booted on that board. Before the patch, with the CPU domain
provider deferring, the boot log shows "failed to create CPU PM domains
ret=-517" followed by "CPU 0 failed to PSCI idle" and "Failed to create
psci-cpuidle device", and /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpuidle/ does not exist.
After it, the provider probes on the retry and both cpuidle states register.
scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict is clean; the patch applies to torvalds master as
of 2026-08-14.

Recipients from scripts/get_maintainer.pl: Lorenzo Pieralisi, Sudeep Holla, Ulf
Hansson, Rafael J. Wysocki, Daniel Lezcano, Christian Loehle, linux-pm,
linux-arm-kernel.
 drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
index dcf20ea5ef5e..ac9dd2ed91cc 100644
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
@@ -489,4 +489,13 @@ static int __init psci_idle_init(void)
 
 	return 0;
 }
-device_initcall(psci_idle_init);
+
+/*
+ * A faux device is probed synchronously and cannot be deferred, so this has to
+ * run after the CPU PM domain provider has had its own probe retried. That
+ * retry is flushed by deferred_probe_initcall(), a late_initcall, which is why
+ * a device_initcall here loses the race whenever the provider depends on a
+ * driver that probes after it - an always-on domain above the CPU clusters,
+ * for instance.
+ */
+late_initcall_sync(psci_idle_init);
